## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design streaming algorithms with bounded memory while computing exact results. It captures a common real-world problem: maintaining aggregate statistics over a sliding or growing dataset without storing all historical values.

## Key Concepts

- **Streaming data structures:** Using heaps to maintain top-K elements efficiently
- **Running sum:** Incremental updates avoid recomputing the sum after each batch
- **Time complexity:** O(log K) per element; batch processing takes O(B log K)
- **Memory usage:** O(K) vs O(N) for the exact method that stores all past values

## Code Example

```javascript
function topKSumStream(valueStream, K) {
  const heap = new MinHeap();
  let sum = 0;

  for (const batch of valueStream) {
    for (const v of batch) {
      if (heap.size() < K) {
        heap.push(v);
        sum += v;
      } else if (heap.size() > 0 && v > heap.peek()) {
        sum += v - heap.pop();
        heap.push(v);
      }
    }
    console.log("Top-K sum:", sum);
  }
}
```

## Asymptotic Analysis

| Metric | Our Solution | Exact Method |
|--------|--------------|--------------|
| Per-element update | O(log K) | O(1) |
| Batch time (size B) | O(B log K) | O(B) |
| Memory | O(K) | O(N) |

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests real-time data tracking with batched updates and sliding window counts. It evaluates the ability to design a data structure that combines efficient timestamp-based aggregation with per-session lookup while managing amortized cleanup.

## Key Concepts

- Batched online updates with timestamp ordering
- Sliding window counts with expiration
- Two-tier data structures (ordered map + hash map)
- Amortized analysis versus naive recalculation

## Code Example

```javascript
class SessionTracker {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.idToTime = new Map();
    this.timeToCount = new Map();
  }

  processBatch(events, currentTime) {
    for (const { sessionId, time, isNew } of events) {
      if (isNew) {
        this.idToTime.set(sessionId, time);
        this.timeToCount.set(time, (this.timeToCount.get(time) || 0) + 1);
      } else {
        const oldTime = this.idToTime.get(sessionId);
        this.timeToCount.set(oldTime, this.timeToCount.get(oldTime) - 1);
        this.idToTime.set(sessionId, time);
        this.timeToCount.set(time, (this.timeToCount.get(time) || 0) + 1);
      }
    }
    this.expireOldSessions(currentTime);
  }

  expireOldSessions(currentTime) {
    const threshold = currentTime - this.W;
    for (const [time, count] of this.timeToCount) {
      if (time < threshold) {
        this.timeToCount.delete(time);
      }
    }
  }

  countActive(currentTime) {
    const threshold = currentTime - this.W;
    let count = 0;
    for (const [time, c] of this.timeToCount) {
      if (time >= threshold) count += c;
    }
    return count;
  }
}
```

## Analysis

**Preprocessing**: O(1) — just initialize empty maps.

**Memory**: O(S) where S is the number of unique timestamps with active sessions; worst case O(N) for N sessions if all have distinct timestamps.

**Update**: Each event is O(log T) for the ordered map insertion/deletion, where T is the number of unique timestamps. With B events per batch, this is O(B log T) amortized.

**Query**: O(T) in the worst case — must scan all timestamps in the window. Using an ordered map with prefix sum augmentation could reduce this to O(log T).

**Comparison to naive recalculation**: Recalculating from scratch after each batch would require O(N) per query, where N is total active sessions. The two-tier structure provides O(1) lookup per session update and O(T) or O(log T) queries — a significant improvement when batches are processed frequently and queries are intermittent.

## Why This Is Asked
This tests basic algorithmic analysis and trade-offs between brute force and divide-and-conquer techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Time complexity and space usage
- Inversions problem and two standard approaches
- Trade-offs with input size and data order

## Code Example
```javascript
function countInversionsNaive(a){
  let c=0;
  for(let i=0;i<a.length;i++){
    for(let j=i+1;j<a.length;j++){
      if(a[i]>a[j]) c++;
    }
  }
  return c;
}
function countInversionsMergeSort(a){
  function sortCount(arr){
    if(arr.length<=1) return {array: arr, inv:0};
    const mid = Math.floor(arr.length/2);
    const left = sortCount(arr.slice(0,mid));
    const right = sortCount(arr.slice(mid));
    let i=0,j=0, merged=[], inv= left.inv + right.inv;
    while(i<left.array.length && j<right.array.length){
      if(left.array[i] <= right.array[j]) merged.push(left.array[i++]);
      else { merged.push(right.array[j++]); inv += left.array.length - i; }
    }
    return {array: merged.concat(left.array.slice(i)).concat(right.array.slice(j)), inv: inv};
  }
  return sortCount(a).inv;
}
```

